1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,012
Shanghai, May 1949.

2
00:00:03,442 --> 00:00:06,692
China's richest city woke up to a strange
sight:

3
00:00:07,122 --> 00:00:10,307
Mao Zedong's soldiers, asleep on its
pavements.

4
00:00:10,737 --> 00:00:12,460
They had just taken the city.

5
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:16,622
By most accounts, they had orders not to
enter a single home.

6
00:00:17,052 --> 00:00:21,337
Twenty-two years earlier, this army was
under a thousand beaten men,

7
00:00:21,507 --> 00:00:23,145
hiding in the mountains.

8
00:00:23,575 --> 00:00:26,409
Fifteen years earlier, it ran for its
life,

9
00:00:26,579 --> 00:00:30,063
and most of those who started that run
never finished it.

10
00:00:30,493 --> 00:00:31,901
Now it held China.

11
00:00:32,331 --> 00:00:34,777
So how did an army that kept running away

12
00:00:34,947 --> 00:00:37,379
win the most populous country on earth?

13
00:00:37,809 --> 00:00:40,818
This is the story of China's guerrilla
war.

14
00:00:44,748 --> 00:00:47,506
Shanghai, April 1927.

15
00:00:47,935 --> 00:00:52,673
China's Nationalist leader, Chiang Kai-
shek, turned on his communist allies.

16
00:00:53,103 --> 00:00:56,781
His soldiers and gangsters hunted them
through the streets.

17
00:00:57,211 --> 00:01:00,478
By most accounts, hundreds were shot in
the first days,

18
00:01:00,648 --> 00:01:04,225
and thousands more across China in the
months that followed.

19
00:01:04,655 --> 00:01:07,742
The communists had tried to win China in
the cities.

20
00:01:08,173 --> 00:01:10,681
In the cities, they had just been
destroyed.

21
00:01:11,110 --> 00:01:15,288
That August, a farmer's son from Hunan
spoke at a secret meeting.

22
00:01:15,718 --> 00:01:17,766
His name was Mao Zedong.

23
00:01:18,197 --> 00:01:21,928
Political power, he said, grows out of the
barrel of a gun.

24
00:01:22,358 --> 00:01:25,764
In September, he led a peasant uprising in
Hunan.

25
00:01:26,195 --> 00:01:28,017
It was crushed within days.

26
00:01:28,447 --> 00:01:32,225
Mao gathered what was left, by most
accounts under a thousand men,

27
00:01:32,394 --> 00:01:35,327
and led them towards the Jinggang
Mountains.

28
00:01:35,758 --> 00:01:38,085
On the way, he gave them three rules.

29
00:01:38,514 --> 00:01:40,017
One: obey orders.

30
00:01:40,447 --> 00:01:43,798
Two: do not take a single sweet potato
from the peasants.

31
00:01:44,228 --> 00:01:46,951
Three: hand over everything taken from the
rich.

32
00:01:47,381 --> 00:01:50,519
Remember that sweet potato. It will matter
later.

33
00:01:52,049 --> 00:01:54,869
The Jinggang Mountains were bandit
country.

34
00:01:55,299 --> 00:01:57,329
Two bandit chiefs ruled them.

35
00:01:57,758 --> 00:02:00,533
Mao recruited them instead of fighting
them.

36
00:02:00,963 --> 00:02:04,667
In April 1928, another survivor arrived.

37
00:02:05,098 --> 00:02:09,152
Zhu De, a former warlord officer, brought
his own battered column.

38
00:02:09,582 --> 00:02:12,259
Together, they formed the Fourth Red Army.

39
00:02:12,689 --> 00:02:16,215
Zhu commanded the troops. Mao was its
political chief.

40
00:02:16,645 --> 00:02:19,831
They were outnumbered, outgunned, and
short of everything.

41
00:02:20,261 --> 00:02:22,535
So they learned to fight a different way.

42
00:02:22,965 --> 00:02:27,247
By May, Mao later wrote, it fitted into
sixteen Chinese characters.

43
00:02:27,677 --> 00:02:29,301
Four lines of four.

44
00:02:29,731 --> 00:02:32,048
The enemy advances, we retreat.

45
00:02:32,477 --> 00:02:34,396
The enemy halts, we harass.

46
00:02:34,827 --> 00:02:36,852
The enemy tires, we attack.

47
00:02:37,282 --> 00:02:39,420
The enemy retreats, we pursue.

48
00:02:39,849 --> 00:02:43,517
Watch these sixteen characters. The whole
war is written in them.

49
00:02:43,947 --> 00:02:45,450
The rules grew too.

50
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:48,958
The sweet potato became a needle and a
thread:

51
00:02:49,388 --> 00:02:51,321
do not take even those.

52
00:02:51,752 --> 00:02:54,429
Pay for what you buy. Return what you
borrow.

53
00:02:55,959 --> 00:03:00,089
By 1930, the Red Army had moved into
southern Jiangxi.

54
00:03:00,519 --> 00:03:02,889
Chiang sent an army to destroy it.

55
00:03:03,319 --> 00:03:05,294
Mao did not stand and fight.

56
00:03:05,723 --> 00:03:09,238
He pulled his men back, deep into villages
that would hide them.

57
00:03:09,668 --> 00:03:12,983
The enemy marched in after them, strung
out and lost.

58
00:03:13,413 --> 00:03:16,474
Then the Red Army struck, one column at a
time.

59
00:03:16,904 --> 00:03:20,638
Chiang tried three times, in 1930 and
1931.

60
00:03:21,069 --> 00:03:22,829
Three times, he failed.

61
00:03:23,259 --> 00:03:27,174
The enemy advances, we retreat. The first
line had worked.

62
00:03:27,604 --> 00:03:32,332
In November 1931, the communists declared
a state of their own:

63
00:03:32,762 --> 00:03:36,795
the Chinese Soviet Republic, with its
capital at Ruijin.

64
00:03:37,225 --> 00:03:40,538
It had its own laws, its own money, and
its own army.

65
00:03:40,968 --> 00:03:43,692
But inside it, something darker was
happening.

66
00:03:44,123 --> 00:03:47,359
Mao's men hunted for traitors in their own
ranks.

67
00:03:47,789 --> 00:03:51,720
Under torture, prisoners named others, who
were tortured in turn.

68
00:03:52,150 --> 00:03:56,441
By most estimates, thousands of soldiers
and party members were shot.

69
00:03:56,871 --> 00:03:59,620
The revolution was already eating its own.

70
00:04:01,151 --> 00:04:05,363
For his fifth attempt, in 1933, Chiang
changed the game.

71
00:04:05,792 --> 00:04:10,976
Advised by German officers, he ringed the
communists with stone blockhouses.

72
00:04:11,406 --> 00:04:14,071
Thousands of them, joined by new roads.

73
00:04:14,501 --> 00:04:17,357
Every month, the ring crept a few miles
closer.

74
00:04:17,788 --> 00:04:20,355
The ring tightened slowly, like a noose.

75
00:04:20,784 --> 00:04:24,597
The Red Army now had new leaders. Mao had
been pushed aside.

76
00:04:25,027 --> 00:04:28,968
Moscow's man was a German communist called
Otto Braun.

77
00:04:29,398 --> 00:04:32,891
He ordered them to hold every hill and
fight in the open.

78
00:04:33,321 --> 00:04:35,352
They fought like a regular army,

79
00:04:35,522 --> 00:04:37,005
and they lost like one.

80
00:04:37,435 --> 00:04:39,231
Their losses were enormous.

81
00:04:39,661 --> 00:04:43,513
By the autumn of 1934, the Soviet was
dying.

82
00:04:43,943 --> 00:04:46,248
The leaders decided to break out.

83
00:04:46,679 --> 00:04:49,029
They did not yet know where they were
going.

84
00:04:50,559 --> 00:04:54,625
In October, over 80,000 people slipped
through the ring at night.

85
00:04:55,055 --> 00:04:58,529
They hauled printing presses and heavy
equipment on their backs.

86
00:04:58,959 --> 00:05:03,128
By some accounts, Mao, sick with malaria,
was carried on a stretcher.

87
00:05:03,558 --> 00:05:06,759
At the Xiang River, the Nationalists
caught them crossing.

88
00:05:07,189 --> 00:05:10,730
By some estimates, the army shrank by half
within weeks.

89
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:15,891
In January 1935, the leaders met in the
town of Zunyi.

90
00:05:16,321 --> 00:05:20,437
Braun's tactics took the blame, and Mao
was brought back into command.

91
00:05:20,867 --> 00:05:25,358
Party history calls it the turning point.
Historians are more cautious.

92
00:05:25,788 --> 00:05:27,796
But the guerrilla rules came back.

93
00:05:28,225 --> 00:05:30,834
The army stopped marching in a straight
line.

94
00:05:31,264 --> 00:05:33,022
It doubled back, feinted,

95
00:05:33,193 --> 00:05:37,079
and crossed the Chishui River four times
to shake off its hunters.

96
00:05:38,609 --> 00:05:42,173
In May, the army reached the Dadu River,
in the far west.

97
00:05:42,603 --> 00:05:47,104
The crossing was the Luding Bridge:
thirteen iron chains over a torrent.

98
00:05:47,534 --> 00:05:50,457
Party history says the planks had been
torn up,

99
00:05:50,627 --> 00:05:54,883
and that a few dozen volunteers crawled
across the chains under fire.

100
00:05:55,314 --> 00:05:58,546
Some historians doubt there was much of a
battle at all.

101
00:05:58,976 --> 00:06:01,157
Either way, the army got across.

102
00:06:01,587 --> 00:06:05,472
Then came the Snow Mountains, climbed in
thin cotton uniforms.

103
00:06:05,902 --> 00:06:09,505
Then the grasslands, swamps where men sank
and never came up.

104
00:06:09,935 --> 00:06:14,522
In October 1935, Mao's column reached
northern Shaanxi.

105
00:06:14,952 --> 00:06:18,361
By most accounts, fewer than ten thousand
of them were left.

106
00:06:18,792 --> 00:06:23,051
Mao called it 25,000 li: about 12,500
kilometres.

107
00:06:23,481 --> 00:06:27,642
Two British walkers who retraced it
measured about 6,000.

108
00:06:28,072 --> 00:06:30,662
Either way, it was a very long way to run.

109
00:06:31,092 --> 00:06:33,973
They had survived. But they were cornered,
and starving.

110
00:06:34,403 --> 00:06:36,700
And Chiang was coming to finish them.

111
00:06:39,830 --> 00:06:44,362
In the summer of 1936, an American
reporter reached their camp.

112
00:06:44,793 --> 00:06:46,714
His name was Edgar Snow.

113
00:06:47,144 --> 00:06:51,120
His book, Red Star Over China, made Mao
famous worldwide.

114
00:06:51,549 --> 00:06:55,155
In December, he flew to Xi'an to order one
last offensive.

115
00:06:55,585 --> 00:06:59,546
But his generals there wanted to fight a
different enemy: Japan.

116
00:06:59,977 --> 00:07:04,726
Japan had seized Manchuria, the homeland
of one of them, Zhang Xueliang.

117
00:07:05,155 --> 00:07:09,688
On the twelfth of December, Zhang's troops
stormed Chiang's quarters before dawn.

118
00:07:10,118 --> 00:07:14,367
By most accounts, China's leader fled up a
hillside in his nightclothes.

119
00:07:14,797 --> 00:07:17,094
He was found hiding among the rocks.

120
00:07:17,524 --> 00:07:20,551
The man who had hunted the communists for
nine years

121
00:07:20,721 --> 00:07:22,175
was now a prisoner.

122
00:07:22,605 --> 00:07:24,768
Two weeks later, he was released.

123
00:07:25,198 --> 00:07:28,868
The communist envoy, Zhou Enlai, helped
broker the deal.

124
00:07:29,298 --> 00:07:33,500
Chiang agreed, in effect, to stop the
civil war and fight Japan.

125
00:07:33,930 --> 00:07:37,969
Zhang Xueliang flew back with him, and
spent decades under arrest.

126
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:40,293
The hunt was over. For now.

127
00:07:41,823 --> 00:07:46,331
In July 1937, Japan invaded China in full.

128
00:07:46,761 --> 00:07:50,443
Chiang's armies fought the great battles,
and lost most of them.

129
00:07:50,873 --> 00:07:55,954
Shanghai fell. Then Nanjing, where
Japanese troops massacred civilians for

130
00:07:56,384 --> 00:08:00,982
The communists' main force, renamed the
Eighth Route Army, went somewhere else:

131
00:08:01,411 --> 00:08:03,403
behind Japanese lines.

132
00:08:03,833 --> 00:08:08,109
In September, at Pingxing Pass, it
ambushed a Japanese column.

133
00:08:08,539 --> 00:08:11,202
It was a small battle, but a famous one.

134
00:08:11,633 --> 00:08:14,927
After that, the guerrillas mostly avoided
big fights.

135
00:08:15,357 --> 00:08:18,486
In a book that year, Mao compared the
people to water,

136
00:08:18,655 --> 00:08:21,168
and guerrillas to the fish that swim in
it.

137
00:08:21,599 --> 00:08:24,276
Japan held the cities and the railways.

138
00:08:24,706 --> 00:08:28,135
The communists held the villages in
between, and recruited.

139
00:08:28,565 --> 00:08:32,026
The enemy halts, we harass. The second
line had worked.

140
00:08:33,556 --> 00:08:37,449
In August 1940, the communists tried
something bigger.

141
00:08:37,879 --> 00:08:42,263
Over a hundred regiments struck Japanese
railways, bridges and mines.

142
00:08:42,693 --> 00:08:45,506
It was called the Hundred Regiments
Offensive.

143
00:08:45,936 --> 00:08:49,803
Japan's answer became known in China as
the Three Alls:

144
00:08:50,233 --> 00:08:52,313
kill all, burn all, loot all.

145
00:08:52,743 --> 00:08:56,337
Villages suspected of helping guerrillas
were wiped off the map.

146
00:08:56,767 --> 00:09:01,782
By some estimates, the population of
communist areas fell by almost half.

147
00:09:02,213 --> 00:09:05,631
Mao drew a lesson: never show your full
strength too early.

148
00:09:06,061 --> 00:09:11,822
In January 1941, Nationalist troops
ambushed the communist New Fourth Army.

149
00:09:12,252 --> 00:09:16,429
From then on, the alliance against Japan
existed in name only.

150
00:09:16,858 --> 00:09:21,276
In Yan'an, Mao purged his own party again,
and rewrote its history.

151
00:09:21,707 --> 00:09:25,360
When Japan surrendered, in August 1945,

152
00:09:25,530 --> 00:09:27,095
the communists were ready.

153
00:09:27,525 --> 00:09:31,916
In 1937, the party had about 40,000
members.

154
00:09:32,346 --> 00:09:34,175
Now it had over a million.

155
00:09:35,705 --> 00:09:39,667
In 1946, the civil war began again, in
full.

156
00:09:40,097 --> 00:09:42,206
On paper, it was no contest.

157
00:09:42,636 --> 00:09:46,477
Chiang had over four million men, many
with American weapons.

158
00:09:46,907 --> 00:09:50,274
The communists had perhaps a million,
counting militias.

159
00:09:50,704 --> 00:09:55,741
In March 1947, Chiang's army captured
Mao's capital, Yan'an.

160
00:09:56,171 --> 00:09:57,749
Mao simply walked away.

161
00:09:58,179 --> 00:10:02,238
The enemy advances, we retreat. The oldest
rule still worked.

162
00:10:02,668 --> 00:10:06,469
Where they ruled, the communists seized
land and handed it out.

163
00:10:06,899 --> 00:10:10,358
Peasants' sons joined them in their
hundreds of thousands.

164
00:10:10,788 --> 00:10:15,909
Chiang's China was rotting: inflation,
corruption, men conscripted at gunpoint.

165
00:10:16,339 --> 00:10:20,976
In Manchuria, the communists besieged the
city of Changchun for five months.

166
00:10:21,406 --> 00:10:23,345
They let almost no one leave.

167
00:10:23,775 --> 00:10:28,054
By most estimates, at least 150,000
civilians starved.

168
00:10:29,885 --> 00:10:32,816
In China, that siege is still rarely
mentioned.

169
00:10:33,246 --> 00:10:37,133
Then, from late 1948, came three giant
campaigns.

170
00:10:37,563 --> 00:10:41,871
In the biggest, Huaihai, over half a
million Nationalist troops were lost.

171
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:45,876
The enemy tires, we attack. The third line
had worked.

172
00:10:47,406 --> 00:10:52,310
In April 1949, a million communist troops
crossed the Yangtze.

173
00:10:52,740 --> 00:10:55,699
Nanjing, Chiang's capital, fell within
days.

174
00:10:56,129 --> 00:11:00,233
The enemy retreats, we pursue. All sixteen
characters, complete.

175
00:11:00,663 --> 00:11:02,714
Then, in late May, Shanghai.

176
00:11:03,144 --> 00:11:05,097
Remember the sweet potato?

177
00:11:05,527 --> 00:11:10,729
Twenty-two years on, the rule faced its
hardest test: China's richest city.

178
00:11:11,159 --> 00:11:15,045
By many accounts, the troops slept on
pavements, not in homes.

179
00:11:15,475 --> 00:11:18,662
Shanghai had expected looting. It woke up
to this.

180
00:11:19,092 --> 00:11:22,387
It was the best advertisement the
communists ever had.

181
00:11:22,817 --> 00:11:27,743
On 1 October 1949, at Tiananmen Gate in
Beijing,

182
00:11:27,913 --> 00:11:30,852
Mao proclaimed the People's Republic of
China.

183
00:11:31,282 --> 00:11:33,690
In December, Chiang fled to Taiwan.

184
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:37,607
The army that had run away for twenty-two
years had won.

185
00:11:39,137 --> 00:11:40,844
But the promise had limits.

186
00:11:41,274 --> 00:11:45,441
In the land reform that followed,
landlords were tried before crowds.

187
00:11:45,871 --> 00:11:49,731
Estimates of the dead run from hundreds of
thousands to millions.

188
00:11:50,161 --> 00:11:54,639
Mao would rule China for twenty-seven
years, at a terrible human cost.

189
00:11:55,069 --> 00:11:57,654
Yet his guerrilla rules escaped China.

190
00:11:58,084 --> 00:12:02,301
In Vietnam, they shaped the war against
France, and then America.

191
00:12:02,730 --> 00:12:05,709
In Peru, the Shining Path called itself
Maoist.

192
00:12:06,140 --> 00:12:10,921
In Nepal, from 1996, Maoists fought a ten-
year "people's war".

193
00:12:11,350 --> 00:12:14,686
By most counts, more than 13,000 people
died.

194
00:12:15,116 --> 00:12:18,414
In 2008, Nepal's monarchy was abolished,

195
00:12:18,584 --> 00:12:21,831
and the guerrilla leader Prachanda became
prime minister.

196
00:12:22,262 --> 00:12:24,920
Sixteen characters, from a mountain
hideout,

197
00:12:25,089 --> 00:12:28,754
are still studied in military academies
around the world.

198
00:12:29,185 --> 00:12:33,248
A guerrilla army needs the people, the way
a fish needs water.

199
00:12:33,678 --> 00:12:35,693
It wins by protecting them.

200
00:12:36,123 --> 00:12:37,507
But once it has won,

201
00:12:37,677 --> 00:12:39,457
who protects the people from it?
